# Here are some notes for a quick start.
#
# The state from which Nix is presently restarted dates from
# Fri Sep  2 22:03:15 CET 2011
#
# The kernel was initially started from the 9k, mainly due to
# Jim McKie [jmk] with possible contributions from others from
# Bell Labs.
#
# The modified Nix one: k10, was the result of the work of
# many others, including (in nicknames alphabetical order):
#
#	esoriano [Enrique Soriano]
#	forsyth [Charles Forsyth]
#	jmk [Jim McKie]
#	nemo [Francisco J. Ballesteros]
#	npe [Noah Evans]
#	paurea [Gorka Guardiola Múzquiz]
#	rminnich [Ron Minnich]	
#
# (these are also the authors of the main articles concerning the
# subject.)
#
# This resulting Nix is:
#	- made for objtype=amd64
# 	- has 2M pages, and supports 1G pages.
#
# The initial version was delivered with a whole (Bell Labs) plan9
# distribution.
#
# Ron Minnich and mainly Paul A. Lalonde have cleaned the distribution
# so that remains, in fact, what is Nix proper (modifications or
# additions).
#
# The first aim is to resurrect this version, but with a 9front
# distribution, the most actively developed and used Plan9 "branch".
#
# Here are quick notes to get you started:
#
# The bulk of the work are modifications against some Plan9
# distribution. Once you're under 9front, you need first to get
# the modifications by cloning this very repository.
#
# We will suppose on the following that you have put the sources
# in $home/nix-os.

# So for example cloning from the master src:
#
cd
git/clone https://github.com/rminnich/nix-os

# Once you get the modifications served, the first step is to
# manipulate the namespace in order for changed files to
# mix with unchanged ones. The 'nix' rc script in
# sys/src/nix/ will take care of that:

cd nix-os/sys/src/nix
nix

# The namespace is now set to allow the compilation and,
# furthermore, rc scripts provided for testing will now be
# accessible as nix/some_script.

#                   COMPILATION OF THE KERNEL
#
cd boot
mk
cd ../k10
mk ../root/nvram
mk

# You should have now (after the commands above) a 9k8cpu kernel
# to test.
#
# It can be tested under qemu or using 9front vmx with the 
# Paul Lalonde's script---arguments passed to the script are
# arguments passed to the kernel by vmx:
#
nix/test_vmx
